<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout component="day16" xml:id="day-16-lecture">
    <title>Day 16</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            Ch 6.2: Shrinkage - Ridge regression
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <objectives>
        <title>Covered in this class</title>

        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    What is regularization?
                    Why do we need it?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    What are the two basic types of regularization methods?
                    How are they implemented mathematically in linear
                    regression?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    How do you fit a ridge regression model in Python?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    How do you control model flexibility and the bias-variance
                    tradeoff when using regularization?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    How do you find the right amount of regularization using
                    cross-validation?
                    How do you do this in Python?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    What additional precautions do you need to take when using
                    regularization compared to least squares?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    What are the advantages of regularization compared to least
                    squares?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    What are the advantages of regularization compared to subset
                    selection?
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>

    <paragraphs xml:id="regularization-goal">
        <title>Goal</title>

        <p>
            <md>
                Y = \beta_0 + \beta_1 X_1 + \beta_2 X_2
          + \beta_3 X_3 + \beta_4 X_4
            </md>
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Fit a model using all <m>p</m> predictors.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Aim to constrain (regularize) coefficient estimates.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Shrink the coefficient estimates toward <m>0</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Ridge regression
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Lasso
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Ridge regression</title>


                <p>
                    Before:
                </p>

                <p>
                    <md>
                        RSS = \sum_{i=1}^{n}
          \left(y_i-\beta_0-\sum_{j=1}^{p}\beta_j x_{ij}\right)^2
                    </md>
                </p>

                <p>
                    After:
                </p>

                <p>
                    <md>
                        \sum_{i=1}^{n}
          \left(y_i-\beta_0-\sum_{j=1}^{p}\beta_j x_{ij}\right)^2
          +\lambda\sum_{j=1}^{p}\beta_j^2
          = RSS+\lambda\sum_{j=1}^{p}\beta_j^2
                    </md>
                </p>



        <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
            <stack>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\lambda \geq 0</m> is a tuning parameter to
                                determine separately.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The second term is called the shrinkage penalty.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\lambda=0</m> gives the least squares estimate.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                This is our first encounter with regularization,
                                the <m>l_2</m> norm, and the general <m>l_p</m> norm.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Regularization constrains the model, reducing
                                its flexibility.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>

            <stack>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                As <m>\lambda\to\infty</m>, the shrinkage penalty
                                has an increasing impact, and the ridge
                                regression coefficients approach zero.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The penalty is not applied to the intercept.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Denote the ridge regression coefficient
                                estimates by
                                <m>\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{\lambda}^{R}
                                =(\hat{\beta}_{1,\lambda}^{R},\ldots,\hat{\beta}_{p,\lambda}^{R})</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Example from the <c>Credit</c> data</title>

        <p>
            <md>
                RSS + \lambda \sum_{j=1}^{p}\beta_j^2
            </md>
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="40% 40%">
            <image source="figures/Fig6-4a.png">
                <shortdescription>
                    Ridge regression coefficient paths for the Credit data as
                    the tuning parameter lambda increases.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>

            <stack>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                At the left end of the x-axis, <m>\lambda \approx
                                0</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Each line represents the predicted coefficient
                                value for a single variable.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                As <m>\lambda</m> increases, all coefficients
                                approach zero, essentially giving the null model
                                at the right end.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Same Setting, Different Plot</title>

        <p>
            <md>
                RSS + \lambda \sum_{j=1}^{p}\beta_j^2
      \qquad
      \|\boldsymbol{\beta}\|_2 =
      \sqrt{\sum_{j=1}^{p}\beta_j^2}
            </md>
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="40% 45%">
            <image source="figures/Fig6-4b.png">
                <shortdescription>
                    Ridge regression coefficient paths plotted against the
                    relative size of the coefficient vector.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>

            <stack>
                <p>
                    <ul >
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The vector <m>\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}</m>
                                contains the least squares coefficient
                                estimates.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The norm <m>\|\cdot\|_2</m> measures the
                                distance from zero.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The ratio
                                <m>\|\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_\lambda^R\|_2/\|\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}\|_2</m>
                                ranges from <m>1</m> when <m>\lambda=0</m> down
                                to <m>0</m> as regularization increases.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                This ratio measures the amount by which the
                                coefficient estimates have been shrunk.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The ratio makes shrinkage more comparable across
                                different regularization methods.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The left side is less flexible; the right side
                                is more flexible.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Scale equivariance (or lack thereof)</title>

        <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
            <stack>
                <p>
                    Scale equivariant:
                </p>

                <p>
                    Multiplying a variable by <m>c</m>, giving <m>cX_i</m>,
                    returns a coefficient multiplied by <m>1/c</m>, giving
                    <m>\beta_i/c</m>.
                </p>
            </stack>

            <stack>
                <p>
                    <ul >
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Example: <c>income</c> variable.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Least squares is scale equivariant.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Ridge regression is not scale equivariant.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>X_j\hat{\beta}_{j,\lambda}^{R}</m> depends
                                not only on <m>\lambda</m> but also on the
                                values of the other predictors.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Solution: Standardize predictors</title>

        <p>
            <md>
                \widetilde{x}_{ij} =
      \frac{x_{ij}}{
      \sqrt{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(x_{ij}-\overline{x}_j)^2}
      }
            </md>
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The denominator is the estimated standard deviation of
                        the \(j\)th predictor.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Standardized predictors will all have a standard
                        deviation of one.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Previous figures showed standardized ridge regression
                        coefficients on the <m>y</m>-axis.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Using Cross-Validation to Find <m>\lambda</m></title>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Choose a grid of <m>\lambda</m> values.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Compute the ( <m>k</m>-fold) cross-validation error for
                        each value of <m>\lambda</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Select the tuning parameter value <m>\lambda</m> for
                        which the cross-validation error is smallest.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Refit the model using all available observations and the
                        selected value of the tuning parameter.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>
            LOOCV Choice of <m>\lambda</m> for Ridge Regression and
            <c>Credit</c> Data
        </title>

        <image source="figures/Fig6-12.png">
            <shortdescription>
                Leave-one-out cross-validation error for ridge regression on the
                Credit data as a function of the tuning parameter lambda.
            </shortdescription>
        </image>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Dashed vertical lines indicate the selected value of
                        <m>\lambda</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        In this case, <m>\lambda</m> is small, so the optimal
                        fit involves only a small amount of shrinkage relative
                        to least squares.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        The dip is not very pronounced, so a wide range of
                        values would give a very similar error.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        In a case like this, we might simply use the least
                        squares solution.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Bias-Variance Tradeoff</title>

        <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
            <stack>
                <image source="figures/Fig6-5a.png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Squared bias in black, variance in green, and test mean
                        squared error in purple for simulated data.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <p>
                    Squared bias (black), variance (green), and test mean
                    squared error (purple) for simulated data.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The horizontal dashed line is the minimum
                                possible test MSE.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>

            <stack>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Bias-variance tradeoff.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Minimum MSE is achieved around
                                <m>\lambda = 30</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                High variance means the MSE for
                                <m>\lambda = 0</m> (least squares) and
                                <m>\lambda = \infty</m> (null model) are
                                essentially the same.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>More Bias-Variance Tradeoff</title>

        <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">

                <image source="figures/Fig6-5b.png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Squared bias in black, variance in green, and test mean
                        squared error in purple for simulated data.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <p>
                    Squared bias (black), variance (green), and test mean
                    squared error (purple) for simulated data.
                </p>
                </sidebyside>

    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Advantages of Ridge</title>

        <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
            <stack>
                <p>
                    <em>Ridge vs. Least Squares:</em>
                </p>

                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                As seen on the previous slide, ridge regression
                                can reduce variance.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Ridge regression works best when the least
                                squares estimates have high variance.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                It can trade a small increase in bias for a
                                large decrease in variance.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>

            <stack>
                <p>
                    <em>Ridge vs. Subset Selection:</em>
                </p>

                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Ridge regression is much more computationally
                                efficient.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                It fits a single model rather than considering
                                <m>2^p</m> possible models.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Computational techniques are available to solve
                                for all values of <m>\lambda</m> simultaneously.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>
</handout>